
<% content_for :title, "Asking for Help | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="help-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="help-hero">

    <picture class="help-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_asking_for_help_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_asking_for_help_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="help-hero-content">

      <p class="help-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / ASKING FOR HELP
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>YOU NEED</span>
        <span>A HAND.</span>
        <span>NOT A DEFENSE</span>
        <span>ATTORNEY.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="help-intro">
        MAKE THE ASK. SKIP THE COURTROOM DRAMA.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE NEED -->
  <section class="help-section help-pink">
    <div class="help-inner">

      <p class="help-number">01 / THE NEED</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DO<br>
        YOU NEED?
      </h2>

      <div class="help-copy">

        <p>
          You're overwhelmed, exhausted,
          frustrated, or struggling with
          something you can't manage alone.
          Fine. But before you start
          explaining yourself to someone,
          figure out what you're actually
          asking for.
        </p>

        <p>
          Do you need practical assistance?
          Someone to listen? Company?
          Professional help with your mood,
          medications, or recovery?
        </p>

        <p>
          Different problems require
          different kinds of help.
          Your friend isn't your psychiatrist,
          and your psychiatrist isn't
          going to carry your groceries.
        </p>

        <p>
          Be specific about the need.
          You don't have to solve
          everything before asking
          someone to help with
          one part of it.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="help-grid">

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>PRACTICAL HELP.</h3>

          <p>
            Rides to appointments,
            groceries, errands,
            carrying heavy things,
            paperwork, or tasks
            your back won't
            comfortably let you do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Figure out what needs
            to happen, when it needs
            to happen, and exactly
            what you're asking
            someone to do.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need to
            explain your entire
            medical history
            to request assistance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>SOMEONE TO LISTEN.</h3>

          <p>
            Sometimes you need
            to talk through something
            without immediately
            being offered solutions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tell the person whether
            you want advice,
            another perspective,
            or simply someone
            to hear you out.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can ask someone
            to listen without
            expecting them to
            fix the problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>COMPANY.</h3>

          <p>
            Maybe you're lonely,
            isolating, or tired
            of spending so much
            time inside your
            own head.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask someone to visit,
            watch a movie,
            talk on the phone,
            or just hang out.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need
            an elaborate reason
            to want company.
            Sometimes that's
            the whole request.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>HELP FROM YOUR DOCS.</h3>

          <p>
            Changes in sleep,
            mood, impulsivity,
            depression, racing
            thoughts, or cravings
            deserve attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contact your psychiatrist or
            therapist
            before symptoms
            become overwhelming.
          </p>

          <p>
            If suicidal ideation
            appears, tell your
            psych and therapist.
            Don't wait until
            you intend to act
            before seeking support.
          </p>

          <p>
            Give them the actual
            information. They
            can't respond properly
            to symptoms you
            haven't mentioned.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THE HESITATION -->
  <section class="help-section help-black">
    <div class="help-inner">

      <p class="help-number">02 / THE HESITATION</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT MAKES<br>
        ASKING SO<br>
        FUCKING HARD?
      </h2>

      <div class="help-copy">

        <p>
          You need something, and suddenly
          you're preparing a legal defense.
          Why you need help. Why you can't
          do it yourself. Why you're
          not being unreasonable.
          Why the other person
          shouldn't be annoyed.
          Why are you so incapable. Why are you so needy.
          Why can't you be more independent.
        </p>

        <p>
          By the time you've finished
          explaining, you've either
          talked yourself out of asking
          or made a simple request
          unnecessarily complicated.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you don't want to
          seem needy. Maybe you hate
          depending on people.
          Maybe you're afraid they'll
          say no, disappoint you,
          or think you're a burden.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes those concerns
          come from experience.
          People can be unreliable,
          break commitments, or
          make helping you feel
          like an inconvenience.
        </p>

        <p>
          But you can't decide
          someone is going to
          reject you before
          you've actually asked.
          That's another prediction
          you're treating like a fact.
        </p>

        <p>
          There's a difference
          between being considerate
          and apologizing for
          having needs.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You're making a request.
            You're not on trial.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="help-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT ARE YOU AFRAID WILL HAPPEN
          IF YOU ASK? HAS THAT ACTUALLY
          HAPPENED, OR ARE YOU ALREADY
          REHEARSING THE REJECTION?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR PEOPLE -->
  <section class="help-section help-yellow">
    <div class="help-inner">

      <p class="help-number">03 / YOUR PEOPLE</p>

      <h2>
        WHO CAN<br>
        YOU ASK?
      </h2>

      <div class="help-copy">

        <p>
          Not everyone is the right
          person for every problem.
          Someone can be a great friend
          and still be terrible at
          showing up on time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Think about what you're asking
          for and who can realistically
          help. Consider availability,
          reliability, experience,
          and whether you feel
          comfortable being honest
          with that person.
        </p>

        <p>
          Have a backup, especially
          when the need is important.
          Depending on one person
          for something essential
          leaves you without options
          if they're unavailable.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="help-grid">

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>WHO FEELS SAFE?</h3>

          <p>
            Who can you be honest with
            without worrying they'll
            mock you, judge you,
            or use your vulnerability
            against you?
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about who respects
            your boundaries, understands
            your sobriety, and can
            listen without making
            everything about themselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need to tell
            every person everything.
            Choose someone appropriate
            for what you're sharing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>WHO CAN HELP WITH THIS?</h3>

          <p>
            Who has the time,
            ability, knowledge,
            or resources to
            provide what you need?
          </p>

          <p>
            A friend might help
            with transportation.
            Someone from Harmony
            might understand a
            drinking urge.
            Your therapist can
            help you work through
            an emotional problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your psychiatrist
            is the person to
            contact about changes
            in mood symptoms
            or psychiatric treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the request
            to the person.
            Don't expect everyone
            to be able to
            do everything.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / THE ASK -->
  <section class="help-section help-purple">
    <div class="help-inner">

      <p class="help-number">04 / THE ASK</p>

      <h2>
        FIND THE<br>
        FUCKING WORDS.
      </h2>

      <div class="help-copy">

        <p>
          Stop building an elaborate
          explanation and make
          the actual request.
        </p>

        <p>
          Say what you need,
          when you need it,
          and what you're asking
          the other person to do.
          Give them enough
          information to answer.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can be polite
          without apologizing
          six times. You can
          acknowledge someone's
          time without explaining
          why you deserve help.
        </p>

        <p>
          And don't make people
          guess. If you need
          a ride, ask for a ride.
          If you need someone
          to listen, say that.
          If you're worried about
          your mood, describe
          what's actually changing.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="help-grid">

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>I NEED A HAND.</h3>

          <p>
            "Hey, I need some help
            with something. My back
            has been making it
            difficult to manage
            on my own. Would you
            be able to help me
            with [task] on [day]?"
          </p>

          <p>
            Specific request.
            Actual timeframe.
            No courtroom defense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>I NEED YOU TO LISTEN.</h3>

          <p>
            "I'm having a rough
            time with something.
            Do you have a few
            minutes to listen?
            I'm not really
            looking for advice.
            I just need to
            talk it through."
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>I DON'T WANT TO BE ALONE.</h3>

          <p>
            "Hey, I've been
            isolating a bit
            and could use
            some company.
            Want to come
            hang out or
            watch something?
            Nothing serious."
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>SOMETHING FEELS OFF.</h3>

          <p>
            "I've noticed some
            changes in my mood
            and sleep, and
            I'm concerned about
            where things might
            be heading.
            Can we talk about
            what's happening
            and what I
            should do next?"
          </p>

          <p>
            Be specific about
            symptoms, timing, and
            medication changes.
            If it's urgent,
            say so directly.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / THE RESPONSE -->
  <section class="help-section help-black">
    <div class="help-inner">

      <p class="help-number">05 / THE RESPONSE</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT IF<br>
        THEY SAY NO?
      </h2>

      <div class="help-copy">

        <p>
          Sometimes people say no.
          Sometimes they don't answer.
          Sometimes they agree to help
          and later discover they can't.
        </p>

        <p>
          That can be frustrating,
          especially when you're
          depending on them.
          You're allowed to
          be disappointed.
        </p>

        <p>
          But don't automatically
          turn one declined request
          into a conclusion that
          nobody cares about you.
          Pay attention to what
          actually happened.
        </p>

        <p>
          There's a difference between
          someone being unavailable
          once and someone repeatedly
          making promises they
          don't keep. You can
          recognize unreliable
          behavior without assuming
          everyone will disappoint you.
        </p>

        <p>
          A no means you need
          another solution.
          It doesn't mean
          you should stop asking.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="help-grid">

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>WHAT DID THEY ACTUALLY SAY?</h3>

          <p>
            Did they explicitly
            refuse? Did they say
            they're unavailable?
            Did they simply
            not respond yet?
          </p>

          <p>
            Separate the response
            from your interpretation.
            Don't invent motives
            or assume rejection
            where you don't
            have the facts.
          </p>

          <p>
            If someone repeatedly
            breaks commitments,
            acknowledge that pattern
            and adjust how much
            you depend on them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="help-box">
          <h3>WHAT'S ANOTHER OPTION?</h3>

          <p>
            Contact another person.
            Ask whether a different
            time would work.
            Find another service
            or practical solution.
          </p>


          <p>
            Don't let one
            unanswered message
            become the end
            of your plan.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="help-callout">
        <p>
          ONE PERSON SAYING NO DOESN'T
          MEAN EVERYONE WILL. WHAT'S
          YOUR NEXT REASONABLE OPTION?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / RECEIVING HELP -->
  <section class="help-section help-pink">
    <div class="help-inner">

      <p class="help-number">06 / RECEIVING HELP</p>

      <h2>
        LET SOMEONE<br>
        SHOW UP.
      </h2>

      <div class="help-copy">

        <p>
          You've asked. Someone said yes.
          Now comes the apparently
          impossible part: actually
          letting them help.
        </p>

        <p>
          Stop apologizing for the
          inconvenience after they've
          already agreed. Stop insisting
          you can do everything yourself
          when the entire reason you
          asked was that you needed
          assistance.
        </p>

        <p>
          If someone offers to carry
          something, let them.
          If someone agrees to give
          you a ride, accept the ride.
          If a friend offers to listen,
          you can talk without repeatedly
          explaining why your problems
          are worth hearing about.
        </p>

        <p>
          Say thank you. Respect their
          time and boundaries. Follow
          through on your part of
          the arrangement.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to immediately
          repay every favor or treat
          accepting assistance like
          signing a contract.
        </p>

        <p>
          And receiving help doesn't
          mean giving someone control
          over your decisions.
          You can accept support
          while maintaining your
          independence and boundaries.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes doing something
          yourself makes sense.
          Sometimes getting help
          makes more sense.
          You don't get extra
          credit for choosing
          the harder option.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            FIGURE OUT WHAT YOU NEED.
            FIND THE RIGHT PERSON.
            MAKE THE REQUEST.
            THEN LET THEM ANSWER.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="help-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT DO YOU NEED HELP WITH
          RIGHT NOW? WHO CAN YOU ASK?
          WHAT WOULD YOU SAY IF YOU
          STOPPED TRYING TO JUSTIFY IT?
        </p>
      </div>
<!-- BACK TO TOOLBOX -->
<nav class="index-home-nav" aria-label="Return to toolbox">
  <%= link_to "← BACK TO TOOLBOX",
      toolbox_path,
      class: "index-home-button" %>
</nav>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
